today we're going to talk about a food  that needs no introduction we're talking  about ice cream hi my name is Anissa  Ramirez welcome to science explained  last year the United States consumed  over a billion and a half gallons of ice  cream that's equal to about 2,300  Olympic size pools or 11 and a half  million bathtubs amazing that's a lot of  ice cream from a science point of view  ice cream is pretty amazing too  first all three states of matter coexist  we have air bubbles a gas liquid of milk  and also we have ice crystals which are  a solid all living together in a yummy  combination now in order to make ice  cream what you need to do is make a  concoction of milk and sugar and  flavoring and mix them all together and  cool them to very very low temperatures  one way that you can do that is with  liquid nitrogen liquid nitrogen is  really cold it's minus 320 degrees  Fahrenheit or minus 230 degrees Celsius  that's as cold as Neptune so let's see  what happens ooh that's neat all right  so if I mix this and continue to add  liquid nitrogen and keep stirring it  I'll eventually get ice cream now  this ice cream is the best ice cream  you're going to get because the ice  crystals are super small because we've  cooled it with really really cold  temperatures and it's going to make the  ice cream really really creamy if you  don't have liquid nitrogen available you  can also make ice cream at home with  things that you have in your  refrigerator you can make a combination  of super cold liquid how do you do that  well you add salt to ice now when you  add salt this is called freezing point  depression this happens all the time  when cities put salt out on the snow  what they're really doing is they're  lowering the freezing point so that  liquid will be present at those  temperatures so what else can we learn  about ice cream  well when you melt ice cream and  refreeze it it's not the same  it changes permanently that's because  the ice crystals  grow to be bigger and bigger and change  the texture now this may not seem very  serious but scientists are interested in  this because this tells us about other  things like avalanches avalanches occur  because snowpack has different crystal  sizes and eventually it gets to a point  where it's unstable and causes snow to  travel at a hundred miles per hour  that's no joke  so you see ice cream has a lot of  lessons in it not only does it tell us  how to create tasty treats but it also  teaches us how to prevent ourselves from  serious threats  I'm Anissa Ramirez thank you for joining  me at signs explained and I hope that  you'll come back for another edition
 

